    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d244">

                <pb facs="0555" n="508" xml:id="pg508"/>

                <head>244. Information Memorandum From the Assistant Secretary of State for
                    Inter-American Affairs (Enders) to Secretary of State Haig<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d244-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1982-05-10">May 10, 1982</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Brazilian Reply to your May 6 letter on the South Atlantic</item>
                </list>
                <p>Attached is an informal translation of Foreign Minister Guerreiro’s May 7
                        reply<anchor n="2" xml:id="d244-f2"/> to your letter of May 6.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d244-f3"/> The reply makes three points worth noting:</p>
                <p>. . . restraint is essential, not only on the part of the U.K. and Argentina, but
                    also of “countries that may help tip the balance of the current military
                    situation . . .”</p>
                <p>. . . the time has arrived to seek a solution involving force withdrawal, a
                    temporary U.N. presence on the islands, and U.N. sponsored negotiations with
                    purposefully vague guidelines on self-determination and sovereignty.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d244-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>. . . Brazil is concerned about the impact of the crisis on inter-American
                    relations.</p>
                <p>Guerreiro’s May 7 letter is his second to you on this issue, both sent in reply
                    to your letters to him.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d244-f5"/> President Figueiredo
                    also replied to President Reagan’s May 4 letter on the crisis.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d244-f6"/> In this exchange of correspondence the Brazilians have
                    made clear their hope that the crisis and associated questions will be explored
                    thoroughly during Figueiredo’s visit. Your breakfast with Guerreiro on May 12
                    may provide the best opportunity for a full discussion.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d244-f7"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d244-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d244-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File, P820069–1749.
                    Confidential. Drafted by Kilday. Haig initialed at the top of the memorandum,
                    indicating that he saw it. A stamped notation also indicates that Haig saw the
                    memorandum.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f2" n="2" xml:id="d244-f2-content">
                <p>Not found attached. The complete texts of the Department’s unofficial translation
                    as well as the Portuguese original of Guerreiro’s May 7 letter are in the
                    Department of State, Central Foreign Policy File, P820076–1145.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f3" n="3" xml:id="d244-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d238">Document 238</ref> and <ref target="#d238-f1">footnote
                        1</ref> thereto.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f4" n="4" xml:id="d244-f4-content">
                <p> Haig underlined “self-determination and sovereignty” and drew a checkmark next
                    to these words.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f5" n="5" xml:id="d244-f5-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d196">Document 196</ref> and <ref target="#d238-f1">footnote 1,
                        Document 238</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f6" n="6" xml:id="d244-f6-content">
                <p>Likely an erroneous reference to Reagan’s May 2 letter to Figueiredo (see <ref
                        target="#d204">Document 204</ref>). For Figueiredo’s May 4 response, see
                        <ref target="#d229-f2">footnote 2, Document 229</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d244-f7" n="7" xml:id="d244-f7-content">
                <p> Haig underlined this sentence and wrote “agree” in the right-hand margin.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
